::: {.doc-subject}

Enumerate all trails (walks with distinct edges) from source to target.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L68363-L68481){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.enumerate_trails(
    source,
    target,
    max_trails=1000,
)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Enumerate trails](../assets/graphs/enumerate-trails.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A directed path A, B, C, D with a loop B, E, F back to B. Both trails from A to D are returned: one goes around the loop (teal) and visits B twice, which a trail allows because no edge repeats; the other is the plain path (accent color)." width="100%"}

Returns the actual sequences of nodes for each trail. A trail is a walk where all edges are
distinct, though nodes may repeat, so a trail can loop through a node more than once as
long as it never reuses an edge. Each trail ends the first time it reaches `target=`.

This is the listing counterpart of `count_trails()`. Use it when you need to inspect the
routes themselves, for example to:

- see which detours make the trail count larger than the simple-path count
- collect candidate routes for redundant routing or edge-covering tours
- summarize route lengths, or check which nodes are revisited

On a directed graph edges are only followed in their own direction; on an undirected
graph each edge can be crossed in either direction, but only once. Trails are returned in
the order the depth-first search finds them, as lists of node IDs. Because the number of
trails can grow exponentially, results are capped at `1000` by default. The method does
not modify the graph.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node the trails start from, given as an integer node ID or a string label.

<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The node the trails end at, given as an integer node ID or a string label. If it is the
    same node as `source=`, the result is a single one-node trail.

<code>[max_trails]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[1000]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Maximum number of trails to return. Default is `1000`. Once this many trails have been
    found the search stops and the partial list is returned, so a result of exactly
    `max_trails=` trails may mean more exist. If `None`, every trail is returned, which can
    take a very long time and a lot of memory on all but small graphs. `0` returns an
    empty list.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[list[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of trails, where each trail is a list of node IDs from source to target (both
    inclusive). If the limit is reached, only the first `max_trails=` trails found are
    returned. Returns an empty list if `target=` cannot be reached.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If source or target node not found in graph.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Same enumeration as `count_trails()`, but every trail is materialized as its full
node sequence, which changes the memory picture entirely: even a modest $1{,}000$
trails at $50$ nodes apiece is several megabytes of Python lists, and the counts
explode faster than the graph size does, so `max_trails=` must almost always be set
to something sane. Because edges are unique but nodes are not, a returned trail can
revisit nodes freely, which is exactly what makes trails useful for reasoning about
redundant routing, network vulnerability, or edge-covering tours where the walker
may pass through a hub several times.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~count_trails`) 

:   Count the trails without listing them.

[](`~all_simple_paths`) 

:   Paths that never revisit a node.

[](`~eulerian_path`) 

:   A trail that crosses every edge exactly once.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A trail can pass through a node twice, as long as it doesn't reuse an edge. Here the
second trail goes around the loop through `B`:

::: {#254fe135 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("B", "E"), ("E", "F"), ("F", "B")],
    directed=True,
)

[[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in t]
 for t in graph.enumerate_trails("A", "C")]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
[['A', 'B', 'C'], ['A', 'B', 'E', 'F', 'B', 'C']]
```
:::
:::


### The longest walks between two islands

`konigsberg()` is the city of Königsberg as Euler studied it in 1736, with seven bridges
joining its two islands and two river banks. There are 25 trails from Kneiphof to Lomse,
and the longest cross five bridges:

::: {#a9a831df .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg = ec.konigsberg()
label = konigsberg.get_label_from_id
trails = konigsberg.enumerate_trails("Kneiphof", "Lomse")
longest = [t for t in trails if len(t) == 6]

len(trails), [label(n) for n in longest[0]]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(25, ['Kneiphof', 'South Bank', 'Kneiphof', 'North Bank', 'Kneiphof', 'Lomse'])
```
:::
:::


Kneiphof is joined to each bank by two bridges, so a trail can cross to a bank and come
straight back. The one above crosses both bridges to the South Bank and both to the North
Bank before taking the Honigbrücke to Lomse:

::: {#d9b6eefe .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
konigsberg.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight=longest[0], color_by="kind",
    label_by="label", edge_label_by="bridge",
)
```

